# XL1 alerting on Windows. Read by scripts\xl1-alert.ps1 from config\alert.env.
#
# The Pi bundle's alert.env, with the same variable names on purpose: an
# operator running both machines configures them the same way, and the two
# alerters report the same conditions under the same keys.
#
# Every channel is optional and all are off until filled in. Set as many as you
# want; each alert goes to all of them.

# --- dead man's switch: the one that catches a PC that stops existing ---------
#
# Every other channel here only reports problems this machine is alive to
# observe. A PC that loses power, blue-screens, or has Docker Desktop killed by
# an update sends nothing -- and nothing is exactly what a healthy PC sends too.
#
# Set this to a URL that an external service expects to be pinged regularly, and
# which alarms when the pings stop. Free options: healthchecks.io (email/SMS/
# push, no card), or a Push monitor in a self-hosted Uptime Kuma.
#
#   XL1_ALERT_DEADMAN_URL=https://hc-ping.com/<your-uuid>
#
# Set the service's period to a few minutes with a grace of ~10; this pings once
# per alert cycle (60s). A node that reports DOWN also pings <url>/fail, so a
# real failure alarms immediately instead of waiting out the grace period.
XL1_ALERT_DEADMAN_URL=

# --- phone push, easiest ------------------------------------------------------
# Install the "ntfy" app (iOS/Android), subscribe to a topic name, put it here.
# No account, no API key. Anyone who knows the topic name can read it, so use a
# long random one -- this is a shared secret, not a username. In PowerShell:
#
#   -join ((1..24) | ForEach-Object { '0123456789abcdef'[(Get-Random -Max 16)] })
#
XL1_ALERT_NTFY_TOPIC=
XL1_ALERT_NTFY_SERVER=https://ntfy.sh

# --- Discord / Slack ----------------------------------------------------------
# Paste an incoming-webhook URL. One payload satisfies both services.
XL1_ALERT_WEBHOOK=

# --- email --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unlike the Pi, Windows has no local sendmail to fall back on, so an address
# alone delivers nothing -- name a relay as well. Send-MailMessage is what is in
# the box; it speaks SMTP over TLS and wants an app password, not your account
# password, for Gmail or Microsoft 365.
XL1_ALERT_EMAIL=
XL1_ALERT_SMTP_SERVER=
XL1_ALERT_SMTP_PORT=587
# Defaults to XL1_ALERT_EMAIL when empty. Most relays reject a From they do not
# own, so set this to the mailbox the credentials below belong to.
XL1_ALERT_SMTP_FROM=
XL1_ALERT_SMTP_USER=
XL1_ALERT_SMTP_PASS=

# --- behaviour ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Name used in every message. Set it if you ever run two of these. Defaults to
# this machine's COMPUTERNAME.
XL1_ALERT_NAME=

# Alerts fire on transitions. An unresolved problem repeats once per this many
# seconds and is otherwise silent. Default 6h.
XL1_ALERT_COOLDOWN=21600

# Chain blocks that may pass with none of ours counted before it is treated as a
# fault. This is the only check that catches a node which is up, healthy and
# simply not winning -- every other signal reads green through it.
#
# Do not set it low. At a ~9% share roughly ten races are lost for every one won,
# so gaps of thirty or forty blocks are ordinary. 90 blocks is about 85 minutes
# and about a 1-in-9000 run of honest bad luck at that share; raise it if yours
# is lower. 0 disables the check.
XL1_ALERT_STALL_BLOCKS=90

# Seconds a container may run without building a single block before that is
# treated as a launch that never entered production at all -- a distinct failure
# from producing and losing. A node in that state passes /livez forever, so the
# container is never unhealthy, never exits, and no restart policy recovers it;
# only an operator does, which is why it pages as urgent.
#
# Generous on purpose. Building needs a pending transaction, and though a live
# chain always has some, a false "restart me" costs more than noticing a quarter
# of an hour later. 0 disables the check.
XL1_ALERT_LAUNCH_GRACE=900

# Where to read status. Change only if the dashboard is on another port, or set
# the token if DASH_TOKEN is configured in dashboard.env.
XL1_ALERT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8088/api/status
XL1_ALERT_TOKEN=
